  "full_response": "### **Microservices Architecture**\nMicroservices is an architectural style that structures an application as a collection of **loosely coupled, independently deployable services**. Each service:\n- Has a **single responsibility** (e.g., user authentication, payment processing, inventory management).\n- Runs in its own **process** and communicates via **well-defined APIs** (REST, gRPC, message queues).\n- Can be **developed, deployed, and scaled independently**.\n- May use **different technologies** (languages, databases) based on needs.\n\n#### **Key Characteristics**\n✔ **Decentralized** – Teams own services end-to-end.\n✔ **Resilient** – Failure in one service doesn’t crash the whole system.\n✔ **Scalable** – Services can be scaled independently.\n✔ **Evolvable** – Easier to update or replace individual services.\n✔ **Technology Diversity** – Different services can use different stacks.\n\n---\n\n### **Monolithic Architecture**\nA monolith is a **single, unified codebase** where all components (UI, business logic, database) are tightly coupled and deployed together.\n\n#### **Key Characteristics**\n✔ **Simple to develop & deploy** (initially).\n✔ **Easier debugging & testing** (single codebase).\n✔ **Performance** – In-process communication is faster than network calls.\n✔ **Consistent tech stack** – No need to manage multiple languages/frameworks.\n\n---\n\n### **Trade-offs: Microservices vs. Monolith**\n\n| **Factor**               | **Microservices** | **Monolith** |\n|--------------------------|------------------|-------------|\n| **Development Speed**    | Slower (distributed complexity) | Faster (simpler setup) |\n| **Scalability**          | High (scale only what’s needed) | Limited (scale entire app) |\n| **Deployment**           | Independent (CI/CD per service) | Single unit (slower releases) |\n| **Fault Isolation**      | High (failures are contained) | Low (one bug can crash everything) |\n| **Technology Flexibility** | High (polyglot persistence) | Low (single stack) |\n| **Performance**          | Lower (network latency, serialization) | Higher (in-memory calls) |\n| **Testing**              | Complex (distributed testing) | Simpler (unit/integration tests) |\n| **Operational Overhead",
